{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue Query is now written in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Things to keep in mind:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query follows "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DefinitelyTyped's support window"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5.6"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and newer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changes to types in this repository are considered "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"non-breaking"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and are usually released as "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"patch"}]},{"type":"text","value":" semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"highly recommended that you lock your vue-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The non-type-related public API of Vue Query still follows semver very strictly."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-inference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Inference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types in Vue Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: Ref<number> | Ref<undefined>\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUBNYRm8JABN6DInAC8KDNlx4AFAglw4nTocMA9APwG4Q7QGl0eAFxwA2lRjoWVALoAaa1t8ADFGFx0ASjUAPjgABXIQYAwAOigvCAAbbnQdAFYIgPFCCKA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: Ref<string> | Ref<undefined>\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUBNYRm8JABN6DInAC8KDNlx4AFAglw4nTodNwAegH4DcIdoDS6PAC44AbSox0LKgF0ANDZ2+ABijK46AJRqAHxwAArkIMAYAHRQ3hAANtzoOgCskYHihhhZ6KwwEYoM0apxNfSpMBAAyjBQwIwA5lHFhJFAA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This works best if your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"any"},{"type":"text","value":" per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Ref<Group[]> | Ref<undefined>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-EA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-narrowing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Narrowing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue Query uses a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"discriminated union type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the query result, discriminated by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"success"},{"type":"text","value":" status to make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" defined:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data, isSuccess } = reactive(\n  useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }),\n)\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  // ^? const data: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUKEixEcKOnqsYwbuiKlylKr3RUA3BImsIzeEgAm9BgBo4wVAGVkrVulSp1AXjkKlK9AAUaFjCeAEA2lQwbjBUALq2AQCUcJ4AfHAACpr26AB08qgQADaqAQCsSVWGkiRwAfZOLm6oKQgScJ1wlgwSnJydAHoA-BKEEkA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reactive()"},{"type":"text","value":" wrapper is what makes this work: it flattens the result into\nplain values, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" stay part of the same discriminated\nunion. Destructuring "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery()"},{"type":"text","value":" directly gives you independent refs, and\nTypeScript cannot carry a narrowing from one ref to another — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"if (isSuccess.value)"},{"type":"text","value":"\nleaves "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.value"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Group[] | undefined"},{"type":"text","value":". Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reactive()"},{"type":"text","value":", narrow the\nvalue ref itself:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n\nif (data.value !== undefined) {\n  data.value\n  // ^? const data: Group[]\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-the-error-field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing the error field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The type for error defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":", because that is what most users expect."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Ref<unknown>\n\nif (error.value instanceof Error) {\n  error.value\n  //     ^? const error: Error\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-OPAEhwGLFVAlVIAQSUKgAolBZjEZtA4nFEFJPkioOi4O84H8pIQgA","children":[{"type":"text","value":"typescript playground"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":" at all, you can specify the type of the error field:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"However, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":", so if you have a subclass like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AxiosError"},{"type":"text","value":" you can use "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"type narrowing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make the error field more specific:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering a global Error"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Register"},{"type":"text","value":" interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultError"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/vue-query'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/vue-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown | null","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-query-and-mutation-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing query and mutation keys"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-the-query-and-mutation-key-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering the query and mutation key types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Also similarly to registering a "},{"type":"link","href":"#registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"global error type"}]},{"type":"text","value":", you can also register a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryKey"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MutationKey"},{"type":"text","value":" type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Array"},{"type":"text","value":" type, so that your keys remain an array."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/vue-query'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/vue-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typesafe-disabling-of-queries-using-skiptoken","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typesafe disabling of queries using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipToken"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using TypeScript, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipToken"},{"type":"text","value":" to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guides/disabling-queries.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling Queries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"type-inference","text":"Type Inference","level":2},{"id":"type-narrowing","text":"Type Narrowing","level":2},{"id":"typing-the-error-field","text":"Typing the error field","level":2},{"id":"registering-a-global-error","text":"Registering a global Error","level":3},{"id":"typing-query-and-mutation-keys","text":"Typing query and mutation keys","level":2},{"id":"registering-the-query-and-mutation-key-types","text":"Registering the query and mutation key types","level":3},{"id":"typesafe-disabling-of-queries-using-skiptoken","text":"Typesafe disabling of queries using skipToken","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nVue Query is now written in **TypeScript** to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n- TanStack Query follows [DefinitelyTyped's support window](https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window) and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript **5.6** and newer.\n- Changes to types in this repository are considered **non-breaking** and are usually released as **patch** semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!).\n- It is **highly recommended that you lock your vue-query package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release**\n- The non-type-related public API of Vue Query still follows semver very strictly.\n\n## Type Inference\n\nTypes in Vue Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself\n\n\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: Ref<number> | Ref<undefined>\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUBNYRm8JABN6DInAC8KDNlx4AFAglw4nTocMA9APwG4Q7QGl0eAFxwA2lRjoWVALoAaa1t8ADFGFx0ASjUAPjgABXIQYAwAOigvCAAbbnQdAFYIgPFCCKA)\n\n\n\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: Ref<string> | Ref<undefined>\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUBNYRm8JABN6DInAC8KDNlx4AFAglw4nTodNwAegH4DcIdoDS6PAC44AbSox0LKgF0ANDZ2+ABijK46AJRqAHxwAArkIMAYAHRQ3hAANtzoOgCskYHihhhZ6KwwEYoM0apxNfSpMBAAyjBQwIwA5lHFhJFAA)\n\n\n\nThis works best if your `queryFn` has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return `any` per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nconst fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Ref<Group[]> | Ref<undefined>\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-EA)\n\n\n\n## Type Narrowing\n\nVue Query uses a [discriminated union type](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions) for the query result, discriminated by the `status` field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. `success` status to make `data` defined:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nconst { data, isSuccess } = reactive(\n  useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }),\n)\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  // ^? const data: number\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUKEixEcKOnqsYwbuiKlylKr3RUA3BImsIzeEgAm9BgBo4wVAGVkrVulSp1AXjkKlK9AAUaFjCeAEA2lQwbjBUALq2AQCUcJ4AfHAACpr26AB08qgQADaqAQCsSVWGkiRwAfZOLm6oKQgScJ1wlgwSnJydAHoA-BKEEkA)\n\nThe `reactive()` wrapper is what makes this work: it flattens the result into\nplain values, so `isSuccess` and `data` stay part of the same discriminated\nunion. Destructuring `useQuery()` directly gives you independent refs, and\nTypeScript cannot carry a narrowing from one ref to another — `if (isSuccess.value)`\nleaves `data.value` as `Group[] | undefined`. Without `reactive()`, narrow the\nvalue ref itself:\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n\nif (data.value !== undefined) {\n  data.value\n  // ^? const data: Group[]\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Typing the error field\n\nThe type for error defaults to `Error`, because that is what most users expect.\n\n\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Ref<unknown>\n\nif (error.value instanceof Error) {\n  error.value\n  //     ^? const error: Error\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-OPAEhwGLFVAlVIAQSUKgAolBZjEZtA4nFEFJPkioOi4O84H8pIQgA)\n\n\n\nIf you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an `Error` at all, you can specify the type of the error field:\n\n\n\n\nHowever, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of `useQuery` will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an `Error`, so if you have a subclass like `AxiosError` you can use _type narrowing_ to make the error field more specific:\n\n\n\n\n### Registering a global Error\n\nTanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the `Register` interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set `defaultError` to `unknown`:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nimport '@tanstack/vue-query'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/vue-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown | null\n```\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## Typing query and mutation keys\n\n### Registering the query and mutation key types\n\nAlso similarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error), you can also register a global `QueryKey` and `MutationKey` type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the `Array` type, so that your keys remain an array.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/vue-query'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/vue-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n\n\n\n## Typesafe disabling of queries using `skipToken`\n\nIf you are using TypeScript, you can use the `skipToken` to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the [Disabling Queries](./guides/disabling-queries.md) guide.\n\n\n\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: typescript\ntitle: TypeScript\nref: docs/framework/react/typescript.md\nreplace:\n  {\n    'React': 'Vue',\n    '@tanstack/react-query': '@tanstack/vue-query',\n    'react-query package version': 'vue-query package version',\n  }\n---\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //    ^? const data: Ref<number> | Ref<undefined>\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUBNYRm8JABN6DInAC8KDNlx4AFAglw4nTocMA9APwG4Q7QGl0eAFxwA2lRjoWVALoAaa1t8ADFGFx0ASjUAPjgABXIQYAwAOigvCAAbbnQdAFYIgPFCCKA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({\n  //      ^? const data: Ref<string> | Ref<undefined>\n  queryKey: ['test'],\n  queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  select: (data) => data.toString(),\n})\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUBNYRm8JABN6DInAC8KDNlx4AFAglw4nTodNwAegH4DcIdoDS6PAC44AbSox0LKgF0ANDZ2+ABijK46AJRqAHxwAArkIMAYAHRQ3hAANtzoOgCskYHihhhZ6KwwEYoM0apxNfSpMBAAyjBQwIwA5lHFhJFAA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\n```tsx\nconst fetchGroups = (): Promise<Group[]> =>\n  axios.get('/groups').then((response) => response.data)\n\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const data: Ref<Group[]> | Ref<undefined>\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-EA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n```tsx\nconst { data, isSuccess } = reactive(\n  useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }),\n)\n\nif (isSuccess) {\n  data\n  // ^? const data: number\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#code/JYWwDg9gTgLgBAbzgVwM4FMCKz1QJ5wC+cAZlBCHAOQACMAhgHaoMDGA1gPQBuOAtAEcc+KgFgAUKEixEcKOnqsYwbuiKlylKr3RUA3BImsIzeEgAm9BgBo4wVAGVkrVulSp1AXjkKlK9AAUaFjCeAEA2lQwbjBUALq2AQCUcJ4AfHAACpr26AB08qgQADaqAQCsSVWGkiRwAfZOLm6oKQgScJ1wlgwSnJydAHoA-BKEEkA)\n\nThe `reactive()` wrapper is what makes this work: it flattens the result into\nplain values, so `isSuccess` and `data` stay part of the same discriminated\nunion. Destructuring `useQuery()` directly gives you independent refs, and\nTypeScript cannot carry a narrowing from one ref to another — `if (isSuccess.value)`\nleaves `data.value` as `Group[] | undefined`. Without `reactive()`, narrow the\nvalue ref itself:\n\n```tsx\nconst { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n\nif (data.value !== undefined) {\n  data.value\n  // ^? const data: Group[]\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\n```tsx\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: Ref<unknown>\n\nif (error.value instanceof Error) {\n  error.value\n  //     ^? const error: Error\n}\n```\n\n[typescript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?#-OPAEhwGLFVAlVIAQSUKgAolBZjEZtA4nFEFJPkioOi4O84H8pIQgA)\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n\n```tsx\nimport '@tanstack/vue-query'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/vue-query' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst { error } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups })\n//      ^? const error: unknown | null\n```\n\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n[//]: # 'TypingMeta'\n[//]: # 'TypingMeta'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n"}
